Id: regex-overflow
Title: A regex that overflows the backtracking limit is an error
Input: Text so long, and of such a shape, that matching one of the
 package's patterns needs more than the 1e9 bytes of backtracking stack
 that every regex call allows, in a call to any exported function. For
 example one string of 50,000,000 characters that is U+00C3 U+00A9
 repeated 25,000,000 times, searched with UTF8_DETECTOR_RE (measured on
 stringi 1.8.7 with ICU 71.1: 40,000,000 characters are matched and
 50,000,000 overflow). Only input tens of millions of characters long,
 in one string, gets near the limit.
Reference: Python's re has no backtracking limit. Run on ftfy 6.3.1 with
 Python 3.13: chardata.UTF8_DETECTOR_RE.search('\u00c3\u00a9' * 25_000_000)
 returns a match with span (0, 50000000), in about half a second.
R: An error of class ftfy_regex_overflow. The message names the exported
 function that was called, says how many strings were being matched and
 the length in characters of the longest, and says to split the text
 into shorter strings. No result is returned for any element of the input.
Decided: docs/adr/0005-regex-and-unicode-data-are-generated-from-a-reference-python.md
